Q: Why does std::vector sometimes reallocate even when I know how many elements I’ll add? A: <blockquote>
<p>How does reserve() affect reallocations and object construction?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A vector has two separate things: a capacity and a size. The size is the number of objects that are currently in the vector. The capacity is the number of objects it <em>can</em> hold before it reallocates.</p>
<p><code>reserve()</code> assures that the capacity is (at least) as large as the number you pass, but it doesn't affect the current size (so it doesn't create any objects).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When should I use reserve() instead of creating the vector with an initial size?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You use <code>reserve()</code> when you want to allocate memory without creating any objects in that memory (yet). Creating with an initial size creates a vector that contains the specified number of objects, not just memory where you'll (later) be able to create objects. If you just specify the size, those objects will all be value initialized. If you want them all to have a specific value instead, you can pass that value and they'll all be constructed as copies of what you pass.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

template &lt;class T&gt;
void print(char const *name, std::vector&lt;T&gt; const &amp;vals) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; name;
    for (auto i : vals) { 
        std::cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot;;
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
}

int main() {
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; a(3);
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; b(3, 5);

    print(&quot;a: &quot;, a);
    print(&quot;b: &quot;, b);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<pre><code>a: 0 0 0 
b: 5 5 5 
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>Is the vector’s growth strategy defined by the C++ standard, or does it depend on the implementation?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's partly prescribed, but partly depends on the implementation.</p>
<p>When it does reallocation, it needs to do a calculation like <code>capacity *= constant;</code>. It's <em>not</em> allowed to do something like <code>capacity += constant;</code>.</p>
<p>But the value of <code>constant</code> is not specified. Common values are 1.5 and 2.</p>
<p>But those are just the common values. In theory, somebody <em>could</em> use 10 or 100 or even 1000.</p>
<p>You do need to do an initial allocation and constant large enough to assure that when you do a reallocation, at least one new element is always added. If you used an initial size of 5 and a constant of 1.00001, when you tried to reallocate, the size wouldn't change, so you'd potentially go into an infinite loop of attempting reallocations, none of which actually increased the size at all.</p>
